{"subjectId":"python","id":"string-methods","title":"String Methods Deep Dive","group":"Strings & Numbers","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>str</code> has ~47 methods. Knowing the right one — <code>partition</code> instead of a regex, <code>casefold</code> instead of <code>lower</code>, <code>removeprefix</code> instead of <code>strip</code> — makes text code shorter and correct.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Remember</div><p>Every method returns a <strong>new</strong> string (or a list / bool / int). Nothing modifies the original — strings are immutable.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-letter-case\"></i>Case conversion</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Does</th><th>Example → result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>lower()</code> / <code>upper()</code></td><td>All lower / upper case</td><td><code>'PyThon'.lower()</code> → <code>'python'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>casefold()</code></td><td>Aggressive lower-casing for <strong>caseless comparison</strong></td><td><code>'Straße'.casefold()</code> → <code>'strasse'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>title()</code></td><td>First letter of every word upper</td><td><code>'hello world'.title()</code> → <code>'Hello World'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>capitalize()</code></td><td>First character upper, <strong>rest lower</strong></td><td><code>'hELLO'.capitalize()</code> → <code>'Hello'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>swapcase()</code></td><td>Invert each letter's case</td><td><code>'Ab'.swapcase()</code> → <code>'aB'</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"Straße\".lower(), \"Straße\".casefold())          # straße strasse\nprint(\"STRASSE\".casefold() == \"Straße\".casefold())    # True\nprint(\"STRASSE\".lower() == \"Straße\".lower())          # False\nprint(\"they're bill's\".title())                      # They'Re Bill'S\nprint(\" \".join(w.capitalize() for w in \"they're bill's\".split()))   # They're Bill's</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>title()</code> treats every non-letter as a word boundary, so apostrophes break it (<code>They'Re</code>). Use <code>capitalize()</code> per word, or <code>string.capwords(s)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Searching</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>If not found</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>s.find(sub[, start[, end]])</code></td><td>Lowest index</td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.rfind(sub)</code></td><td>Highest index</td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.index(sub)</code> / <code>s.rindex(sub)</code></td><td>Like <code>find</code> / <code>rfind</code></td><td>raises <code>ValueError</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.count(sub)</code></td><td>Number of <strong>non-overlapping</strong> occurrences</td><td><code>0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.startswith(p)</code> / <code>s.endswith(p)</code></td><td><code>bool</code> — <code>p</code> may be a <strong>tuple</strong> of options</td><td><code>False</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>sub in s</code></td><td><code>bool</code> — the most readable test</td><td><code>False</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>path = \"reports/2026/q1.final.csv\"\nprint(path.find(\"/\"), path.rfind(\"/\"), path.find(\"zip\"))   # 7 12 -1\nprint(path[path.rfind(\".\") + 1:])                         # csv\nprint(path.endswith((\".csv\", \".tsv\")))                    # True\nprint(\"aaaa\".count(\"aa\"), \"2026\" in path)                 # 2 True\nprint(\"banana\".find(\"a\", 2), \"banana\".find(\"a\", 2, 3))    # 3 -1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Use <code>find</code> when \"not found\" is a normal outcome (check for <code>-1</code>); use <code>index</code> when absence is a bug and an exception is what you want. For a plain yes/no, <code>in</code> beats both.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Splitting family</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>s.split()</code></td><td>Split on <strong>runs of any whitespace</strong>, dropping empty strings and leading/trailing space</td></tr><tr><td><code>s.split(sep)</code></td><td>Split on an exact separator — keeps empty strings between adjacent separators</td></tr><tr><td><code>s.split(sep, maxsplit)</code></td><td>At most <code>maxsplit</code> splits, from the left</td></tr><tr><td><code>s.rsplit(sep, maxsplit)</code></td><td>At most <code>maxsplit</code> splits, from the <strong>right</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.splitlines()</code></td><td>Split on <code>\\n</code>, <code>\\r\\n</code>, <code>\\r</code>… without a trailing empty item</td></tr><tr><td><code>s.partition(sep)</code></td><td>Always a 3-tuple <code>(before, sep, after)</code> — at the <strong>first</strong> <code>sep</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s.rpartition(sep)</code></td><td>Same, at the <strong>last</strong> <code>sep</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"  a   b  c \".split())               # ['a', 'b', 'c']\nprint(\"a,,b,\".split(\",\"))                  # ['a', '', 'b', '']\nprint(\"k=v=w\".split(\"=\", 1))               # ['k', 'v=w']\nprint(\"a.b.c.txt\".rsplit(\".\", 1))          # ['a.b.c', 'txt']\nprint(\"one\\ntwo\\r\\nthree\\n\".splitlines())  # ['one', 'two', 'three']\nprint(\"one\\ntwo\\n\".split(\"\\n\"))            # ['one', 'two', '']\nprint(\"user@example.com\".partition(\"@\"))   # ('user', '@', 'example.com')\nprint(\"no-at-sign\".partition(\"@\"))         # ('no-at-sign', '', '')\nkey, _, value = \"timeout=30\".partition(\"=\")\nprint(key, value)                          # timeout 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>partition</code> never raises and always returns three parts — perfect for <code>key, _, value = line.partition('=')</code> parsing without an <code>if</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Trimming &amp; prefixes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"  hi  \".strip() + \"|\")               # hi|\nprint(\"xxhixyx\".strip(\"xy\"))                # hi   ← a SET of characters, not a substring\nprint(\"www.python.org\".lstrip(\"w.\"))        # python.org\nprint(\"test_login.py\".removesuffix(\".py\"))  # test_login\nprint(\"mississippi\".rstrip(\"ip\"))           # mississ\nprint(\"mississippi\".removesuffix(\"ippi\"))   # mississ\nprint(\"reader.txt\".rstrip(\".txt\"))          # reader   ← looks right…\nprint(\"rat.txt\".rstrip(\".txt\"))             # ra       ← …but eats the 't' of 'rat'\nprint(\"rat.txt\".removesuffix(\".txt\"))       # rat</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Classic bug</div><p><code>strip</code>/<code>lstrip</code>/<code>rstrip(chars)</code> remove <strong>any combination of those characters</strong>, not a prefix/suffix string. To remove an exact prefix or suffix use <code>removeprefix</code> / <code>removesuffix</code> (3.9+).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-align-center\"></i>Padding &amp; alignment</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"[\" + \"hi\".center(8, \"*\") + \"]\")      # [***hi***]\nprint(\"[\" + \"hi\".ljust(6) + \"]\")            # [hi    ]\nprint(\"[\" + \"hi\".rjust(6, \".\") + \"]\")       # [....hi]\nprint(\"42\".zfill(5), \"-42\".zfill(5))        # 00042 -0042\nprint(\"a\\tbc\\td\".expandtabs(4))             # a   bc  d\nprint(\"-\" * 10)                             # ----------</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>For tables and reports, f-string alignment (<code>f'{x:&gt;8}'</code>) does the same job inline — see <em>String Formatting</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-replace\"></i>Replacing &amp; translating</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>s = \"a-b-c-d\"\nprint(s.replace(\"-\", \"+\"))                  # a+b+c+d\nprint(s.replace(\"-\", \"\", 2))                # abc-d   ← only the first 2\ntable = str.maketrans({\"a\": \"4\", \"e\": \"3\", \"o\": \"0\"})\nprint(\"hello world\".translate(table))       # h3ll0 w0rld\nstrip_vowels = str.maketrans(\"\", \"\", \"aeiou\")   # 3rd arg: characters to delete\nprint(\"programming\".translate(strip_vowels))    # prgrmmng\nprint(\"abc\".translate(str.maketrans(\"abc\", \"xyz\")))   # xyz</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>translate</code> does many single-character replacements (and deletions) in <strong>one pass</strong> — much faster than chaining <code>replace</code> calls.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checkbox\"></i>Character-class predicates</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>True when every character is…</th><th><code>'²'</code></th><th><code>'½'</code></th><th><code>'٣'</code> (Arabic 3)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>isdecimal()</code></td><td>a decimal digit usable by <code>int()</code></td><td>False</td><td>False</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td><code>isdigit()</code></td><td>a digit, including superscripts</td><td>True</td><td>False</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td><code>isnumeric()</code></td><td>any numeric character, incl. fractions</td><td>True</td><td>True</td><td>True</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>for s in [\"123\", \"²\", \"½\", \"٣\", \"-5\", \"3.5\", \"\"]:\n    print(repr(s), s.isdecimal(), s.isdigit(), s.isnumeric())</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>'123' True True True\n'²' False True True\n'½' False False True\n'٣' True True True\n'-5' False False False\n'3.5' False False False\n'' False False False</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"abc123\".isalnum(), \"abc\".isalpha(), \"ABC\".isupper())    # True True True\nprint(\" \\t\\n\".isspace(), \"\".isspace())                          # True False\nprint(\"my_var\".isidentifier(), \"2fast\".isidentifier())          # True False\nprint(int(\"٣\") + 1)                                             # 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>None of the digit predicates accept <code>'-5'</code> or <code>'3.5'</code>. To check \"is this a number?\", just try <code>int(s)</code> / <code>float(s)</code> and catch <code>ValueError</code> (EAFP). Also note all predicates return <code>False</code> for the empty string.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Comparison, repetition &amp; membership</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"apple\" &lt; \"banana\", \"Zebra\" &lt; \"apple\")     # True True   ← 'Z' (90) &lt; 'a' (97)\nprint(sorted([\"b\", \"C\", \"a\"]))                   # ['C', 'a', 'b']\nprint(sorted([\"b\", \"C\", \"a\"], key=str.casefold)) # ['a', 'b', 'C']\nprint(\"10\" &lt; \"9\", int(\"10\") &lt; int(\"9\"))          # True False\nprint(\"ab\" * 3, \"-\" * 0 == \"\")                   # ababab True\nprint(\"ell\" in \"hello\", \"\" in \"hello\")           # True True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>String comparison is <strong>lexicographic by code point</strong>: uppercase sorts before lowercase and <code>'10' &lt; '9'</code>. Sort with <code>key=str.casefold</code> for human order and convert numeric strings before comparing.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Building strings efficiently</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Collect pieces in a list and call <code>''.join(parts)</code> once — O(total length). <code>join</code> needs strings: <code>', '.join(map(str, nums))</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>s += piece</code> inside a big loop — each <code>+=</code> may copy the whole string (O(n²) worst case). CPython sometimes optimises it, but don't rely on it.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>nums = [3, 1, 4]\nprint(\", \".join(map(str, nums)))      # 3, 1, 4\nprint(\"\".join(reversed(\"stressed\")))  # desserts\ntry:\n    \", \".join(nums)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__)           # TypeError</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview questions</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between find and index?</summary><div><p>Both return the lowest index of a substring. <code>find</code> returns <code>-1</code> when it's missing; <code>index</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. Lists only have <code>index</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does 'rat.txt'.rstrip('.txt') return 'ra'?</summary><div><p><code>rstrip</code> takes a <em>set of characters</em> and strips any of them from the end — <code>t</code>, <code>x</code>, <code>.</code> — until it hits a character not in the set. Use <code>removesuffix('.txt')</code> to remove an exact suffix.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>lower() vs casefold()?</summary><div><p><code>casefold()</code> is a stronger normalisation meant for caseless matching: it also maps characters like <code>ß</code> → <code>ss</code>. Use <code>casefold()</code> when comparing user text case-insensitively; <code>lower()</code> for display.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you reverse the words of a sentence?</summary><div><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>s = \"  the sky  is blue \"\nprint(\" \".join(reversed(s.split())))   # blue is sky the</code></pre></figure><p><code>split()</code> with no arguments discards extra whitespace, so the result has single spaces.</p></div></details></section>"}